{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792265497,"nonce":"pzGjlPhFVxf1HaeBHTo6Ds+XAKc=","title":"Python: augmented assignment on an instance ignores __iadd__","message":"`x += y` with `x` an instance calls the class's `__add__` (through `combine_in_place` -\u003e `arithmetic`), never `__iadd__`, so an in-place method that mutates and returns `self` is skipped and the name is rebound to a new object:\n\n    class Acc(object):\n        def __init__(self):\n            self.items = []\n        def __add__(self, other):\n            r = Acc(); r.items = self.items + [other]; return r\n        def __iadd__(self, other):\n            self.items.append(other); return self\n    a = Acc(); b = a\n    a += 1; a += 2\n    print(a is b, a.items, b.items)\n\nCPython prints `True [1, 2] [1, 2]`; zypy prints `False [1, 2] []`. Lowering and inference (`Typer::operation`, used by both `l op r` and `l op= r`) must try the in-place method first, falling back to the forward and then the reflected method, and inference must type `l op= r` by the same method lowering calls.","files":null}]}